          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does antibiotic stewardship guidance say about upper respiratory infections?",
          "answer": "Antibiotic stewardship guidance on upper respiratory infections (from the document \"Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship and when antibiotics are indicated\") says the following:\n\n- Most acute upper respiratory infections are viral. These include the common cold, most sore throats, acute bronchitis, and uncomplicated early sinusitis.\n- Antibiotics do not shorten these infections. They also do not prevent complications at a rate that would justify routine use.\n- Antibiotics can cause harm. The guidance lists side effects, allergic reactions, disruption of normal gut bacteria, *C. difficile* infection, and the spread of antibiotic-resistant bacteria.\n- Not prescribing antibiotics is the recommended approach for these cases. It is not just the absence of a treatment.\n- Clinicians are encouraged to explain the expected course of illness, how long it usually lasts, and what signs would mean the plan should change. They are also encouraged to offer symptom relief.\n\nThe search did not return anything about specific symptoms that would call for antibiotics, specific treatment choices, or what to do if symptoms get worse. Those would need to be discussed with a clinician. If you have severe trouble breathing, chest pain, confusion, or are unable to swallow fluids, seek urgent medical care now.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
